Introduction
The lot size code in many MQL5 examples answers one question. How many lots lose X money if this stop loss is hit? That question is enough for an EA that holds one position on one symbol. My EAs usually trade several symbols at once, and then the one question is not enough. Before a new entry I also want to know how much money is already at risk in the open positions. I also want to know whether the free margin can carry the new trade and whether the symbol's own volume limits allow it. When the volume comes out smaller than expected, I want to know which of these checks made it smaller.
Until now the sizing in my EAs was a few lines copied into each one, and the copies drifted apart. Some of those copies multiplied the stop distance by SYMBOL_TRADE_TICK_VALUE. On the MetaQuotes-Demo server, that formula asks for 10 times the volume that OrderCalcProfit() gives on XAUUSD and XAGUSD (see the Python consistency check below). I moved the sizing into one include file so that each EA uses the same code.
CRiskBudget takes a symbol, a direction, an entry price, and a stop loss. The class returns a volume and the name of the cap that set it. There are five caps: the risk per trade, what is left of an open risk budget for the whole account, a share of the free margin, SYMBOL_VOLUME_MAX, and SYMBOL_VOLUME_LIMIT. The loss per lot comes from OrderCalcProfit(), with an optional slippage buffer. The commission added to it gives the risk per lot. When the volume is 0, the class reports the reason.
The article walks through the class. It then covers a test script with 30 checks, a script that prints a quote for each Market Watch symbol, and a Strategy Tester EA that trades seven symbols with the class. A short Python script then recomputes the tester results from the CSV files the EA writes, as a consistency check. In the Strategy Tester run on MetaQuotes-Demo, the planned open risk after each of the 195 entries stayed within the 3% budget. The planned open risk is the loss at the stops, measured from the open prices. The realized loss of a stop can be larger, and the budget does not limit it. In the two Strategy Tester runs described below, the budget did not reduce the maximum drawdown.
What is already published
Lot sizing and risk limits are not a new subject on this site. The two closest articles are Part 5 (
basket risk
) and Part 6 (
portfolio risk
) of the series by Solomon Anietie Sunday. In Part 5 the demo EA adds up what the stops of the basket legs would cost. However, that basket is one symbol traded by one EA. Part 6 covers the whole account, but it limits the current floating loss and does not use the stop levels. CRiskBudget sizes each new trade by the combined stop loss risk of all positions and pending orders on the account. With its default magic filter, this total includes every symbol, every EA, and manual trades.
The five caps
Each cap turns a limit into a volume. The smallest of the five wins, and it is rounded down to SYMBOL_VOLUME_STEP. If the result is below SYMBOL_VOLUME_MIN, the trade is refused with that cap's name.
