The loss per lot is the money that OrderCalcProfit() returns for 1 lot from the entry to the stop, moved by the slippage buffer. The risk per lot is that loss plus the commission of both sides. The RISK and OPEN_RISK caps divide money by the risk per lot, and the SRiskQuote structure described below keeps both numbers.
The open risk is the loss the account would take if every open position closed at its stop loss. A pending order with a stop loss counts as if it had been filled first. I measure it from the open price of each position, not from the current price. The risk percentage is a share of the balance, and the balance changes by the loss from the open price. A stop that has been moved past the open price locks in a profit. It counts as 0 when that profit covers the commission still to be paid. A position without a stop has no defined risk. By default the class then refuses each new quote until that position has a stop. The total can't be computed while one of its parts is unknown. The same holds for a position or order whose loss OrderCalcProfit() can't calculate. The class then refuses the quote with the status NO_CALC, and a failed calculation is not counted as 0.
The documentation describes SYMBOL_VOLUME_LIMIT as the maximum aggregate volume of an open position and pending orders in one direction. On the MetaQuotes-Demo server, EURUSD and USDJPY have it at 0. A value of 0 means no limit. AUS200 has 15.
How Quote() decides the volume
The smallest of the five caps wins
Fig. 1. The order of the checks in Quote(): the stop and protection checks first, then the loss per lot, the five caps, the smallest cap, and the minimum volume
