Two enums describe the result. ENUM_RB_CAP names the cap that set the volume, and ENUM_RB_STATUS says whether the volume can be sent.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Caps on the volume of one trade. The smallest one sets it        |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
enum
ENUM_RB_CAP
  {
   RB_CAP_RISK=
0
,
// risk per trade
RB_CAP_OPEN_RISK,
// what is left of the open risk budget
RB_CAP_MARGIN,
// allowed share of the free margin
RB_CAP_VOLUME_MAX,
// SYMBOL_VOLUME_MAX
RB_CAP_VOLUME_LIMIT
// SYMBOL_VOLUME_LIMIT minus the volume in use
};
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Result of a quote                                                |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
enum
ENUM_RB_STATUS
  {
   RB_OK=
0
,
// the volume can be sent
RB_BELOW_MIN,
// the smallest cap is below SYMBOL_VOLUME_MIN
RB_BAD_STOP,
// no stop loss, or a stop on the wrong side
RB_NO_CALC,
// OrderCalcProfit() or OrderCalcMargin() failed, also for an open position
RB_UNPROTECTED
// an open position or order has no stop loss
};
StatusText() and CapText() return the enumerator name without its RB_ or RB_CAP_ prefix. The logs and the CSV files use that form, for example NO_CALC for RB_NO_CALC.
A quote keeps everything it found. The raw volume of each cap is kept too, before rounding, so an EA or a test can see how close the other caps were.
