// the cap that set the volume
double
volume;
// volume to send, 0 when rejected
double
loss_per_lot;
// OrderCalcProfit() loss, entry to the stop plus slippage, 1 lot
double
cost_per_lot;
// commission for both sides, 1 lot
double
risk_per_lot;
// loss_per_lot+cost_per_lot, what 1 lot loses at the stop
double
risk_money;
// allowed loss of one trade
double
trade_risk;
// volume*risk_per_lot
double
open_risk;
// open risk before this trade
double
budget;
// open risk budget in money
double
margin;
// OrderCalcMargin() for the volume
double
margin_allowed;
// free margin this trade may use
double
caps[RB_CAPS];
// raw volume of each cap, before rounding
double
tv_ratio;
// tick value loss / OrderCalcProfit() loss, 1.0 when they agree
int
unprotected;
// open positions and orders without a stop loss
int
unpriced;
// open positions and orders whose risk could not be calculated
};
The class has seven settings. Five setter methods configure them. The risk per trade and the open risk budget are percentages of the balance, and the margin share is a percentage of the free margin. The commission is entered per lot and per side. Brokers quote it both ways, and per side is the smaller unit. The slippage buffer moves the stop further away by that many ticks for the loss calculation only.
The magic filter decides whether the open risk counts all positions on the account or only the EA's own. I leave it at -1, all positions, because the money at risk belongs to the account and not to one EA. The seventh setting decides what happens when a position has no stop. The class has one more field, m_tv_tolerance. The field is not a setting, because it holds the 1% tolerance of the tick value check and has no setter.
